            {
               "text": "### Bantu Expansion\n\nWhen we study the languages spoken in Africa, one fact stands out: Africa is huge, yet it has fewer languages than the Americas (*Abya Yala*) and Asia (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\nWhy so few? One explanation is that the Bantu people spread across many parts of Africa long ago (<htcite2>).\n\n#### Two models of expansion\n\nThe figure in this section shows two hypotheses about the Bantu migration (<htcite3>; <htcite4>):\n- **Early-split**: the Bantu migration goes in two directions — east and west across the African continent.\n- **Late-split**: in this hypothesis, the Bantu people disperse only after crossing the rainforests.\n\nIn science, ideas are tested with questions and evidence — they are not accepted just because they sound convincing. So the foundations of this model are not set in stone. Doctor Igor Yanovich raised several challenges, which we look at next (<htcite5>).\n\n#### A linguistic perspective\n\nIgor Yanovich's first point is about how Bantu languages are classified today. He notes that the 540 languages currently grouped into families follow a classification system that is not a final scientific consensus (<htcite6>; <htcite7>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "baffbb52-eb8f-41ee-8842-91b89ae393bb",
                     "text": "Bostoen, K.",
                     "page": "pp. 227",
