            },
            {
               "text": "Another point raised by Igor Yanovich concerns where the Bantu languages may have spread from — Cameroon — roughly between <htdate0> and <htdate1>. He argues that this hypothesis does not yet have enough data to be firmly supported (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).\n\nHe also questions the “family tree” that would connect the Bantu languages. Two issues need deeper study: (1) the sample of data is small, and (2) the links between languages may not go in one direction only — they could converge, as sometimes happens in family-tree analyses (<htcite3>).\n\n#### Archaeology\n\nArchaeology in Africa — especially in the central region — is still young, and few sites have been explored. Also, African soil does not preserve objects well over thousands of years. As a result, we still lack clear evidence of how pottery developed over time (<htcite4>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "baffbb52-eb8f-41ee-8842-91b89ae393bb",
                     "text": "Bostoen, K.",
                     "page": "pp. 228",
